From: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
Subject: [PATCH] b43: AC-PHY: LOFT LUT base is 0 on 2.4 GHz

The base that rxiqcal_coeff_tables_reset() writes into the LOFT LUTs
0x42/0x62/0x82, and that rxcal_afe_finalize_gain_luts() adds the
calibrated LO word onto, is a step function of the channel's pa5g group,
taken from the 5 GHz captures. On 2.4 GHz it was computed from
"5000 + 5 * channel" as well, which puts 5 GHz steps into cores 1 and 2.

The stock driver uses a base of 0 there. A bus trace of wl on a BCM4360
at 2.4 GHz ch 6 (femctrl 3) has both passes, in the order b43 already
runs them: the fill writes each core's bare calibrated word (0x0001,
0x0101, 0xfe00 = the LO words committed to IQLOCAL) to all 128 entries,
the reset writes 0 to all 128 entries of all three LUTs. On 5 GHz the
same trace matches the existing table (0x82: 0xf8fc up to 0x20, then
0xf6f2).

This replaces an earlier workaround of ours that forced the LUTs to 0 on 2.4 GHz:
the end state is the same, but the fill pass now carries the LO word, as
in wl, while the RX IQ measurement iterations that run between the two
passes use it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ac.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ac.c
@@ -7669,12 +7669,21 @@
  *
  * Why the base exists, why the step falls where it does and why it follows
  * the pa5g partition is not known; only that it does.
+ *
+ * The table is a 5 GHz one. On 2.4 GHz the base is 0 on every core: the
+ * stock driver writes the bare calibrated word in the fill pass (0x0001,
+ * 0x0101 and 0xfe00 on a BCM4360 at ch 6, the words it committed to
+ * IQLOCAL) and zero in the reset pass, on all 128 entries of all three
+ * LUTs. A pa5g group taken from "5000 + 5 * channel" has no meaning there.
  */
 static u16 b43_phy_ac_loft_lut_base(struct b43_wldev *dev, unsigned int core,
 				    unsigned int off)
 {
-	unsigned int grp = b43_phy_ac_pa5g_group(dev,
-			5000 + 5 * dev->phy.ac->cal_channel);
+	unsigned int grp;
+
+	if (b43_current_band(dev->wl) == NL80211_BAND_2GHZ)
+		return 0x0000;
+	grp = b43_phy_ac_pa5g_group(dev, 5000 + 5 * dev->phy.ac->cal_channel);
 
 	if (core == 0)
 		return 0x0000;
